<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Cayor</h1><p class="st-meta">1549 CE – 1886 CE · capital Mboul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Wolof kingdom of the coast between Dakar and Saint-Louis, whose last great ruler Lat Dior fought the French railway.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Cayor was a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/jolof-empire">Jolof Empire</a> until 1549, when its governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amari-ngone-sobel-fall">Amari Ngoone Sobel Fall</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buurba-jolof">Buurba Jolof</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-danki">Danki</a> and became the first independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damel">Damel</a>. The capital was later fixed at Mboul. Cayor controlled the coast between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-vert">Cap-Vert</a> peninsula and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> river.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damel">Damel</a> was elected from the Fall royal lineage by a council of great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a>, and ruled with an army of crown slaves, the tyeddo. Cayor traded slaves, hides and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundnut">groundnuts</a> with the French at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/goree">Gorée</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-louis-senegal">Saint-Louis</a>. Kings often also ruled the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-baol">Kingdom of Baol</a>, and tension grew between the tyeddo and Muslim clerics.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 1860s the French began to annex Cayor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damel">Damel</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lat-dior">Lat Dior Diop</a> opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-louis-senegal">Saint-Louis</a> railway and was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dekhele">Dekhle</a> in 1886, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> absorbed the kingdom into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a>. Lat Dior is a national hero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cayor.jpg" alt="Cayor" width="320" height="166"><figcaption>Image: Cheikh Khadim Rassoul D. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_Of_Cayor2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1549 CE – 1886 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mboul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Amari Ngoone Sobel Fall, Lat Sukaabe Fall, Lat Dior</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam and Wolof traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Wolof</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/senegal">Senegal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Cayor, Kajoor, Kayor</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1718&amp;focus=cayor" aria-label="Cayor on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4224 1608 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cayor lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4544 1864L4587 1861M4544 1864L4548 1883M4544 1864L5054 728M4544 1864L4523 1888"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4587 1861h0M4548 1883h0M5054 728h0M4523 1888h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4544 1864h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4544 1864h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1718&amp;focus=cayor">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
